---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About — OG Toolser"
  description="What OG Toolser does, how calculator formulas are chosen and tested, our editorial and correction policy, and how to reach us."
  canonicalPath="/about/"
>
  <div class="container static-page">
    <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: 'About', path: '/about/' }]} />
    <h1>About OG Toolser</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      OG Toolser builds free, no-signup calculators and converters for home improvement, Islamic
      finance and calendar conversion, developer data formats, academic grading, everyday
      questions, and document conversion. Every tool runs in your browser and shows a result the
      moment you finish typing.
    </p>

    <h2>What OG Toolser does</h2>
    <p>
      Each tool on this site targets one specific, well-defined calculation or conversion —
      such as converting CGPA to a percentage, working out tile quantities with waste allowance,
      or calculating zakat against the nisab. We'd rather maintain a smaller set of tools that
      work correctly than a large set we can't stand behind.
    </p>

    <h2>How we choose calculator formulas</h2>
    <p>
      Where a calculation has one broadly accepted method — like alcohol by volume from gravity
      readings, or the AKC's size-adjusted dog-age model — we use that method and document the
      formula on the tool's page. Where methods genuinely differ by institution or school of
      thought (grade-to-percentage conversions, zakat nisab basis), we say so explicitly instead
      of picking one silently, and where possible we let you choose the method or enter your own
      multiplier. We don't invent formulas or present a single approach as universally official
      when it isn't.
    </p>

    <h2>How tools are tested</h2>
    <p>
      Before a calculator is published, its formula is checked against hand-worked examples and,
      where one exists, a documented reference method (e.g. the standard homebrew ABV formula, the
      sitemaps.org protocol for the sitemap validator, or fast-xml-parser's documented conversion
      convention for the XML/JSON tools). Converters and parsers are checked against edge cases —
      empty input, malformed input, repeated elements, multi-document files — so errors are
      reported clearly instead of failing silently. This is manual engineering review, not a
      third-party audit or a formal QA program.
    </p>

    <h2>Editorial and review policy</h2>
    <p>
      Tool pages and guides are written and maintained by the <strong>OG Toolser Editorial
      Team</strong>. We don't publish a named personal byline because the site isn't run by a
      single individual, and we won't invent one. Pages that touch religious, financial, or
      academic guidance (zakat, dog age, grade conversions) carry a visible "last reviewed" date
      and a disclaimer distinguishing the calculation from professional, scholarly, or veterinary
      advice — see each tool's FAQ and footer note for specifics and sources.
    </p>

    <h2>Correction and update policy</h2>
    <p>
      If a formula, conversion, or piece of copy is wrong, we fix it — corrections take priority
      over new features. Content is revisited when we become aware of an error, a source changes
      (e.g. an updated nisab convention or a new grading-scale rule from a university), or a
      user reports a problem. We only change a page's "last reviewed" date when the underlying
      content was meaningfully updated, not on a fixed schedule.
    </p>

    <h2>How to report an error</h2>
    <p>
      Spotted a wrong result, a broken link, or an outdated figure? Tell us on the
      <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> with the tool name and the input you used — we read
      every report.
    </p>

    <h2>Privacy approach</h2>
    <p>
      Every calculator and converter processes your input locally in your browser; we don't
      operate a backend that receives what you type or upload. See the
      <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a> for the full detail, including analytics and
      advertising.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Questions, bug reports, or tool suggestions: see the <a href="/contact/">Contact page</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .static-page {
    max-width: var(--tool-width);
    padding: var(--space-8) 0 var(--space-10);
  }
  .lead {
    font-size: var(--text-lg, 17px);
    color: var(--color-text-muted, #56606d);
  }
  .static-page h2 {
    margin-top: var(--space-8, 32px);
  }
</style>
